l of course there are still  other protections in place for example  the border controls  we're going to have a more proportionate  and balanced test and trace system  and so there are other things that will  continue  to protect us but the most important  thing is the vaccine and i would urge  everyone to make sure that they're  jabbed up  strangely enough i'm off for my second  job later on today so the advice is well  taken  but but just in terms of that thank you  i i suspect i might need it getting up  at half past two tomorrow morning  but just in terms though of of of where  we go next i mean  you yourself have conceded there will be  additional deaths increased infections  means increased death i'm wondering if  we can get a figure  what would be an acceptable excess  mortality rate from covert 19 as we move  forward  it is i have to it's not about you know  something  some number and that's being acceptable  of course not you know what what we are  seeing is that with rising case numbers  and yesterday  i said we could see a doubling of case  numbers by the time we get to  july the 19th we are not seeing a  corresponding increase in  hospitalizations  and and death numbers and that is  because the vaccine's working that's  what the vaccines  for and we can't live in a world where  the only thing that we're thinking about  is covid and not about all the other  health problems not about our economic  problems our educational challenges  and we have to make use of a vaccine  that is thankfully  working and and and be aware of all the  other health problems  i've just mentioned a moment ago but as  health secretary i tell you when i came  into this department with a fresh set of  eyes  it is shocking when you look at all the  other health problems that have built up  some seven million people have not come  forward during the pandemic for help  from the nhs  with their health problems seven million  people now just think about  all the health problems that have been  stored up and  and we need to treat that as a priority  too it cannot just be about covid  sure indeed and as you mentioned the  fresh pair of eyes that you bring to it  i mean  it has to be said your approach this  that which we heard from you yesterday  and the prime minister is is a country  mile away from  from what we were hearing from from the  previous health sector you have to  consider it is a different approach and  that frankly  if matt hancock had remained in post we  probably wouldn't have had an  announcement of the type  we had yesterday so i'm just wondering  what has changed i mean the sage advice  has remained the same between thursday  and today  the you know the advice in terms of the  the data set  has all been going in exactly the same  direction to my mind the only thing that  has changed  is that we all had to swallow a gallon  of mind bleach after we all got to see  the former health secretary grabbing his  aides backside i mean  where is the science that has led us to  making this  fairly fundamental change in approach  i think what's changed since this  pandemic is  obviously we've learned a lot more about  the virus itself and  i've said a lot about the vaccine but  also it's worth mentioning the  treatments are  much much better than they were at the  start of this year one of my first  visits i think on my  second day on the job was to saint  thomas's hospital  and uh and i went to the see the doctors  and the consultants in the wards where  they  were previously treating hundreds of  cobit patients  and now they're at this the time i went  there are hardly any but what they  also explained to me was the impact of  the drugs the treatment  uh that was now available especially  dexmethazone  and that's important too so that's  something that's changed  since the pandemic but most of all  what's changed and again  i have to come back to the it is the  vaccines they are working and it is  allowing us  uh to take the measures that we