nd i think that's  what the prime minister is emphasizing  that's  something that we'd all like to see just  in terms of  what might happen later on in the year  we heard of course from chris whitty the  prime minister and yourself  i've all said that winter may be  difficult it may be difficult for the  health service  on that basis can we just be absolutely  crystal clear  the government retains the power to  reintroduce  restrictions later in the year if  necessary if  as i'm sure you would say absolutely  necessary  the one one concern that is uh still  there and this is  throughout the world is that there will  be new variants i think  no one can rule out there won't be  further uh variants of  of covid and no one will know what they  will look like  there is a risk and i think it's a real  risk of some kind of vaccine resistant  variant there's no sign of that yet  anywhere but but  i think it's something that no one can  rule out and that is why  it's sensible to retain some powers and  one of the things i said yesterday for  example that local authorities  will retain their powers at least until  the end of september and before that  date we will  review them again so if there's a need  for the local use of powers you know  that that is something  uh that can be done uh promptly and so  it's it's important i think to to remain  cautious  in that way it's why we will also retain  uh border controls  uh your viewers will be familiar with  the so-called sort of traffic light  system  that will stay in place there may be  some proportion changes  to that i think the transport secretary  has already said  that we can soon afford to treat people  that are double vaccinated traveling  to amber countries differently but that  will also provide a degree of protection  and then also  there'll be a more proportioned and  balanced test and trace system  so i think all of these uh taken  together uh  are there to continue to protect us  especially against the emergence  